<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kostroma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kostroma is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, at the mouth of the Kostroma river, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Founded in the 12th century, traditionally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky">Yuri Dolgoruky</a>, it was the seat of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> principality of the Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> lands before passing to Moscow in the 14th century. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipatiev-monastery">Ipatiev Monastery</a> is linked with the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> in 1613, and the city became a centre of the linen trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kostroma.jpg" alt="Kostroma" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexxx1979 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kostroma._Fire_Tower_P7140241_2640.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
